2. Five High-Impact Performance Optimizations for Your Stack

1. Zero-Copy Arrow Transfers (DuckDB \(\leftrightarrow \) Polars):
Never convert DuckDB results to Pandas (.df()) or Python dicts (.fetchall()) in your live Silver/Gold pipeline. Always use con.sql(...).pl(), which passes memory pointers directly via Apache Arrow with zero serialization overhead.
2. Pre-Allocated Ring Buffers & orjson:
Use orjson (written in Rust, ~5–10x faster than standard json) for all Redis Stream payloads and Gold state envelopes, and bound every queue.Queue (maxsize=250_000) and Redis Stream (MAXLEN ~ 500000) so a network burst never triggers an Out-of-Memory crash on your 32 GB machine.
3. Disable Python Garbage Collection on the Hot Execution Path:
In execution_router.py, call gc.disable() after startup initialization and trigger manual gc.collect() only during quiet 1-minute boundaries (or between 15:30 and 09:15 IST). This eliminates random 10–30 ms stop-the-world GC pauses right as a breakout order fires.
4. CPU Core Pinning (When Migrating to Ubuntu Linux):
On Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, set CPUAffinity= in your systemd unit files to pin kite_ingestor and execution_router onto dedicated physical CPU cores, preventing OS scheduler cache misses during market-open (09:15 IST) tick bursts.
5. Pytest Parallelization (pytest-xdist):
You already have pytest-xdist>=3.8.0 installed—always run your test suite with uv run pytest -n auto so test workers spawn across all CPU cores simultaneously.

3. What Not to Mix (Common Python Concurrency Pitfalls)

• Never combine fork with multi-threaded libraries (Polars, DuckDB, NumPy): Always keep mp.set_start_method("spawn", force=True) (which we added in main.py). Using Linux's default fork after DuckDB or Polars spawns its internal C++/Rust thread pools causes silent deadlocks.
• Never use ProcessPoolExecutor inside the live 1-second hot path: Spawning processes or pickling large DataFrames across process boundaries takes 15–50 ms—keep hot-path state in Redis (or a single-process memory cache) and reserve multiprocessing for long-lived top-level workers and parallel backtest sweeps.
